Nobody has sysadmin permissions and the previous DBA has left. The client needs a backup. What would you do?

On-Prem SQL Server
        │
        ├── SA account exists
        ├── DBA accounts exist
        └── BUT...
             No one has sysadmin access

First, I would not try to bypass security. 
I would obtain authorized Windows administrator access to the SQL Server host through the organization's emergency access process. 
Then, if necessary, I would start the SQL Server instance in single-user mode and connect using Windows authentication to regain administrative access. 
I could then enable or reset the sa account, or preferably create an appropriate DBA login or Windows group and grant the required server-level permissions. 
After restoring administrative access, I would restart SQL Server normally, connect again, perform the database backup, and verify the backup using RESTORE VERIFYONLY.
===============================
Flow:

Old DBA leaves
       ↓
No sysadmin login available
       ↓
Cannot connect to SQL Server
       ↓
Client needs backup
       ↓
Get authorized Windows Administrator access
       ↓
Start SQL Server in Single-User Mode
       ↓
Connect using Windows authentication
       ↓
Restore administrative access
       ↓
Enable/reset SA OR create proper DBA login
       ↓
Restart SQL Server normally
       ↓
Connect normally
       ↓
BACKUP DATABASE
       ↓
RESTORE VERIFYONLY
